course: dictionary
lesson: 5223
title: Jeer: mocking words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To jeer is to shout or laugh at someone in a rude, mocking way, to show you do not like them. The word is about being unkind. «Toad is dragged off in chains, shrieking, across the market-place where people throw carrots and jeer.» The crowd is being cruel to Toad, so jeer means mocking him.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Mocked and jeered at by stoats and weasels» uses two words that support each other: mocked also means laughed at unkindly. Ask yourself: would a kind person do this? If not, jeer means something unkind.
explain: Jeer can be a verb or a noun. As a verb, you jeer (you do it). As a noun, a jeer is the rude shout or remark itself. Phrases like «jeer at» and «boos and jeers» show the same idea: people are being unkind to someone.
explain: Crowds often jeer at a team or a referee in sport. When a group jeers at one person, it tells that person that everyone is against them. That is why jeering is a kind of bullying, and why kind people do not join in.
fact: To jeer means to shout or laugh at someone in a rude, mocking way.
fact: A jeer is a rude, mocking shout or remark.
fact: Phrases to know: «jeer at» someone, and «boos and jeers».
fact: Jeering as a group, such as a crowd at a match, can be a kind of bullying.
fact: Long ago, people locked in the stocks could be jeered at, and neighbours sometimes threw rotten food at them.
exercise: «The crowd began to jeer at the referee.» What does jeer mean here? | Clap for him warmly | *Shout at him in a rude, mocking way | Ask him for a photo | Whisper a secret to him
exercise: What is a jeer? | A kind, gentle song | *A rude, mocking shout | A quiet, sleepy yawn
exercise: Dana says jeer is just a fancy word for cheer. Why is she wrong? | Jeer and cheer mean exactly the same | Jeer means to clap | It is a longer word | *Jeer is rude and mocking, but cheer is kind and happy
test: In «The crowd gave a loud jeer», what kind of word is jeer? | A colour | A number | A verb, an action you do | *A noun, a thing
test: Why is jeering at someone a kind of bullying? | It helps them learn to swim | It makes them feel warm and loved | *It makes them feel unwanted, as if the whole group is against them | It is a kind of game
exam: «Would you have him a common laughing-stock, mocked and jeered at by stoats and weasels?» What is the Rat asking? | Do you want him to be your best friend? | *Do you want him to be laughed at and mocked by everyone? | Do you want him to win a prize? | Do you want him to go to school?
